/*! \page conventions Conventions

This document summarizes various naming conventions used for the project.

\section conv_vernum Version Numbers

The convention for version numbers is xx.yy.zzsn where:

xx is the major number \n
yy is the minor number \n
zz is the minor-minor number \n
s  is a letter indicating the status of the release - a(lpha), b(eta), or f(inal) \n
n  is an ordinal number \n

For example, the number identifying the latest version of %ObjectHandler is
1.42.0f0.

The corresponding release may be referred to in the format Rxxyyzzsn, where R
stands for release.  For example, the release corresponding to version 1.42.0f0
is R014200f0.

\section conv_binaries Binary Names

Binary files that are output by the project - lib files, XLLs, executables -
are named according to the conventions specified by the boost project:

<a href="http://www.boost.org/more/getting_started/windows.html#library-naming">http://www.boost.org/more/getting_started/windows.html#library-naming</a>

For example, when you compile %ObjectHandler version 1.42.0 using Visual Studio
solution file %ObjectHandler.sln, project ohxllib, configuration Release (static
runtime), platform x64, the output file is
ObjectHandler\lib\ObjectHandler-xllib-v145-x64-mt-s-1_42_0.lib.

\section conv_builds Build Systems

Three build systems are used across the projects.  The table below summarizes
the platforms and compilers that each one currently supports.  In this table
<b>QuantLib</b> refers to the QuantLib library, which is maintained separately
(upstream), while <b>QuantLibAddin et al</b> refers to the four projects that are
distributed together in this repository: gensrc, %ObjectHandler, QuantLibAddin
and QuantLibXL.

<table border="1">
<tr><td><b>Build system</b></td><td><b>QuantLib</b></td><td><b>QuantLibAddin et al</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Visual Studio solution files (Windows)</td><td>VS 2010 - 2026 (toolsets v100 - v145)</td><td>VS 2022 and 2026 only (v143, v145)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CMake (Windows and POSIX)</td><td>Win32 and x64</td><td>x64 only (at present)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Automake / Autoconf (POSIX)</td><td>Still maintained (builds the C++ libraries)</td><td>Present, but not updated for the latest release</td></tr>
</table>

The Visual Studio solution files are maintained by hand.  Each project now
provides a single solution file (for example <tt>%ObjectHandler.sln</tt>); the
platform toolset is selected automatically from the version of Visual Studio used
to open it, so there is no longer a separate solution file per compiler version.

\section conv_boost Boost

All of the projects depend on Boost.  The minimum version of Boost required by
QuantLib is <b>1.58.0</b>.  This release of QuantLibAddin was tested using Boost
<b>1.83.0</b>.

\section conv_runtime Visual C++ Runtime Libraries

The runtime library is a setting in Visual C++, found under Project Properties /
Configuration Properties / C/C++ / Code Generation / Runtime Library:

<img src="images/runtime_library.jpg">

The project files provide a separate configuration corresponding to each
version of the runtime library.  The table below summarizes the available
configurations.

<table border="1">
<tr><td><b>Configuration</b></td><td><b>Runtime Library</b></td><td><b>Compiler Flag</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Release</td><td>Multi-threaded DLL</td><td>/MD</td></tr>
<tr><td>Release (static runtime)</td><td>Multi-threaded</td><td>/MT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debug</td><td>Multi-threaded Debug DLL</td><td>/MDd</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debug (static runtime)</td><td>Multi-threaded Debug</td><td>/MTd</td></tr>
</table>

All of the lib files linked into a given binary (.exe, .dll, .xll) must be compiled
against the same runtime library.

If you compile your project against the DLL version of the runtime library
then the resulting binary will have a dependency on the Microsoft runtime DLLs.

If you are using multiple XLLs linked dynamically at runtime, these must be compiled
against a DLL version of the runtime library e.g. Release.

*/

